  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$U$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in U$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $f(x)\\in U$ but  $\\alpha f(x)\\notin U.$ Indeed, we have that $f(0)=1$ and $2f(0)=2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of functions is commutative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of functions satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$  for every $x\\in R$, satisfies $f(0)=1.$ Thus, it is an element of $U.$ $\\,✓$",
